# Generated token output.
#
# packages/tokens/dist/ is the COMMITTED distribution artifact the sync engine copies
# verbatim into product repos. It is assembled deterministically (stable walk order,
# LF-normalized) and guarded by `pnpm tokens:dist:check`. Formatting it would rewrite
# those bytes and fail that check on the next run — never let Prettier near it.
packages/tokens/dist/
build/

# Canonical AI assets are provenance-stamped, hash-locked generated output.
# Formatting consumer copies would create drift from jrmoulckers/.github.
#
# This is not a style preference. The sync engine hashes the bytes it writes; if they
# no longer match it cannot tell "the member edited this" from "canon changed", so it
# refuses to overwrite and only warns. A single `pnpm format` over these paths would
# therefore stop Studio receiving updates to them, permanently and quietly. That is
# the state jrmoulckers/finance is in today: its vendored tokens.css sat at 20,889
# bytes against canon's 45,465 while the warning went to a log nobody read.
#
# Anything containing a `studio:base` managed region belongs here too, even though
# only part of the file is canon — Prettier cannot format around a region, and
# rewriting inside the markers is drift. `scripts/validate-prettier-ignore.mjs`
# enforces that and fails the build if a marker-bearing file is missing.
#
# Which of these Prettier can actually damage depends on whether it can infer a
# parser. Markdown it parses, so `.github/copilot-instructions.md` was a real
# `format:check` failure — table padding and `*em*` -> `_em_`. The entries below
# with no parser are declarations of ownership, not fixes for an observed break.
# The distinction matters most for AGENTS.md: it is Markdown and a managed-region
# file in canon, but has no region in Studio yet. The day sync adds one, Prettier
# will rewrite inside the markers. That is the case the guard exists for.
.github/agents/
.github/instructions/
.github/prompts/
.github/skills/
.github/copilot-instructions.md
.gitattributes
.studio-sync.lock.json

# Canon Prettier cannot parse. `agency.toml` and `.gitattributes` (above) have no
# inferred parser, so `prettier --check .` skips them silently — they are listed to
# declare that the bytes are upstream-owned, and to stay safe if either is ever named
# explicitly or a TOML plugin is added. Naming such a file on the CLI *is* a hard
# error ("No parser could be inferred"), which is easy to mistake for a repo-wide
# failure when reproducing CI locally with an explicit file list. It is not one.
agency.toml

# Lockfile — owned by pnpm.
pnpm-lock.yaml

# Dependencies and caches.
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/
.turbo/
